**Part A, homepage and footer (Developer A)**
- **Switch:** Flights / Hotels switches the homepage between `/?mode=flights` and `/?mode=hotels`. The back button and shared links work, and the right mode loads first with no flash. Eleven sections swap their content.
- **Animation:** the swap closes small blinds over the cards and reopens them on the new content, staggered, about 0.8 seconds. With reduced motion it swaps instantly.
- **Kept:** the hero and the two entry cards stay as they are, with four small fixes.
- **New lower sections, in order:**
  - recent searches;
  - Recommended for you, from the visitor's searches and views in the browser, with a first-visit default from London;
  - a deal banner with two cabin windows, then 8 deal cards;
  - trending routes or cities;
  - browse by region or property type;
  - cheapest months to fly, or stays for this weekend;
  - how booking works;
  - the trust panel;
  - 8 FAQs per mode;
  - a city directory where each city opens to priced routes or hotels.
- **Footer:** 6 columns and 42 links. Every link goes to an existing page or a real search. A QA step checks every link and anchor.

**Part B, inner pages (Developer B)**
- P1 and P2 depth for every page you listed, each with a 390 version.
- Flight results get a 7-day price strip, Best / Cheapest / Fastest tabs, 7 sorts and 10 filter groups.
- Fares get side-by-side fare columns using the window's teal edge.
- Hotel detail gets all 10 sections you named, with a sticky section nav.
- The 72 hotels without full catalogue records get a defined shorter page, so there are no empty sections.

**Identity:** Overwing stays its own design. The window shape carries through the review score badge, the price strip and the deal banner. Competitor looks are ruled out by name, and seven conflicts between the competitors, your signature moves and the anti-slop rules are listed in the spec. Mock data is aligned to what the catalogue has so far: London is the only origin, it has 6 regions, and 12 cities have hotels (8 each). Prices are shown as "typical $300" in words, never struck through. The spec has no em dashes and no invented client facts; the Chief Officer removed a support response time I had made up.

